# =============================================================================
# ArmInferX — Comprehensive .gitignore
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. PYTHON
#    Why: Python compiles .py files into bytecode .pyc files in __pycache__/.
#    These are machine-specific and must never be committed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.pyo

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. PYTHON PACKAGING / DISTRIBUTION
#    Why: setuptools/pip generate these during `python setup.py sdist` or
#    `pip install -e .`. They are build artifacts, not source.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.egg
*.egg-info/
*.whl
*.tar.gz
*.zip
eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
*.spec

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. PYTHON TESTING / COVERAGE
#    Why: pytest cache, coverage data, mypy cache, and tox working dirs are
#    all regenerable local state.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
#    Why: venv/conda environments contain platform-specific binaries and
#    thousands of installed packages. They are reconstructed from
#    requirements.txt or pyproject.toml.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.python-version
.python-version-dev

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. NODE.JS / NPM
#    Why: node_modules/ is enormous (hundreds of MB), platform-specific, and
#    fully reconstructible from package-lock.json via `npm ci`.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. REACT / VITE BUILD OUTPUT
#    Why: Vite compiles JSX/TSX into optimized bundles in dist/. These are
#    build artifacts served in production, not source.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
dist/
dist-ssr/
*.local

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. VITE / FRONTEND CACHE
#    Why: Vite dev server and build cache are local performance optimizations.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES / SECRETS
#    Why: .env files contain API keys, database URLs, and credentials.
#    Committing these is a critical security risk. Only .env.example
#    (with placeholder values) should ever be committed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. CERTIFICATES / PRIVATE KEYS
#    Why: TLS certs and private keys must never be in version control.
#    A leaked key compromises the entire security of the system.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. DOCKER
#     Why: Docker builds produce large intermediate images and compose may
#     generate runtime state. The Dockerfiles themselves are committed;
#     the artifacts they produce are not.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.docker/
docker-compose.override.yml

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. IDE / EDITOR — VS Code
#     Why: VS Code stores workspace-specific settings (extensions, debug
#     configs, local history) in .vscode/. These are personal preferences
#     that differ between developers and should not be shared.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. IDE / EDITOR — JetBrains (PyCharm, IntelliJ, WebStorm)
#     Why: Same reason as VS Code — project-specific IDE state is personal
#     and not portable across machines.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
*.iml
*.iws
*.ipr
out/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. OS-GENERATED FILES
#     Why: macOS, Windows, and Linux each generate metadata files that
#     have nothing to do with the project source.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 14. PROJECT-SPECIFIC — MODELS
#    Why: ML model files (ONNX, GGUF, TFLite, PyTorch .bin) are hundreds
#    of MB to multiple GB. They belong in cloud storage (S3, HuggingFace)
#    or a model registry, never in git.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
models/*.onnx
models/*.gguf
# Nested model dirs too (models/gguf/*.gguf): `git add .` must never stage the
# ~2 GB Q4_K_M GGUF or the ~6.5 GB FP16 shards (STEP 15 cleanup audit).
models/**/*.gguf
models/**/*.onnx
models/*.tflite
models/**/*.tflite
models/*.bin
models/**/*.bin
models/*.pt
models/**/*.pt
models/*.pth
models/**/*.pth
models/*.safetensors
models/**/*.safetensors
models/*.h5
models/**/*.h5
models/*.pb
models/**/*.pb
models/downloaded/
# HuggingFace download cache left inside models/gguf/.cache (artifact only).
models/**/.cache/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 15. PROJECT-SPECIFIC — BENCHMARK RESULTS
#     Why: Results are regenerable data artifacts (CSV, JSON, logs).
#     Storing them in git bloats history. They should be exported to
#     cloud storage or a database for persistence.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
results/
benchmark-output/
*.csv
*.log

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 16. PROJECT-SPECIFIC — LARGE DATA
#     Why: Training data, datasets, and audio/image samples are too large
#     for git. Use DVC, S3, or HuggingFace Datasets instead.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/
datasets/
*.parquet
*.arrow
*.record

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 17. PROJECT-SPECIFIC — ARM CROSS-COMPILATION
#     Why: Cross-compilation toolchains and sysroots are platform-specific
#     and large. Managed separately, not committed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
toolchains/
sysroot-*/
